1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:12,362
For nineteen days, guests at the Cecil Hotel in downtown Los Angeles drank the water, showered in it, and made their morning coffee with it — without knowing a decomposing body was floating in the tank above them.

2
00:00:12,312 --> 00:00:15,275
That body belonged to Elisa Lam.

3
00:00:15,275 --> 00:00:22,937
Elisa was twenty-one, a University of British Columbia student from Vancouver traveling solo down the California coast.

4
00:00:22,937 --> 00:00:25,900
She called her parents every single day.

5
00:00:25,900 --> 00:00:30,762
She kept a Tumblr blog where she wrote honestly about her struggles with depression.

6
00:00:30,762 --> 00:00:36,900
She was buying gifts for her family at a downtown bookstore on the last day anyone saw her alive.

7
00:00:36,900 --> 00:00:49,900
She was staying at the Cecil Hotel — a fifteen-story building on Skid Row with a documented history of at least sixteen non-natural deaths, and a former address of both Richard Ramirez and serial killer Jack Unterweger.

8
00:00:49,900 --> 00:00:57,050
On January 31st, 2013, hotel surveillance captured Elisa inside an elevator.

9
00:00:57,050 --> 00:01:05,162
For two and a half minutes she pressed every button, hid in the corner, and peered around the door frame as if watching for someone who wasn't there.

10
00:01:05,162 --> 00:01:09,000
The doors stayed open for an unexplained amount of time.

11
00:01:09,000 --> 00:01:14,250
Tens of millions of people have studied this footage and no one agrees on what it means.

12
00:01:14,250 --> 00:01:24,512
Here is the detail that doesn't fit: that same afternoon, a bookstore manager half a block away described Elisa as outgoing and cheerful, buying gifts for her family.

13
00:01:24,512 --> 00:01:29,025
Two versions of the same person, same day, same hour.

14
00:01:29,025 --> 00:01:33,425
The LAPD searched the Cecil multiple times with dogs.

15
00:01:33,425 --> 00:01:37,225
She was fifteen floors above them the entire time.

16
00:01:37,225 --> 00:01:50,312
To reach the rooftop tank herself, she would have had to bypass an alarmed door, climb a ten-foot ladder, and lift a twenty-pound hatch — during what investigators believe was a severe episode of untreated bipolar disorder.

17
00:01:50,312 --> 00:01:54,050
Police say the hatch was closed when the body was found.

18
00:01:54,050 --> 00:01:57,500
The maintenance worker who found her says it was open.

19
00:01:57,500 --> 00:02:03,137
Discovery only came because guests complained about the water pressure and a strange taste.

20
00:02:03,137 --> 00:02:13,375
Nineteen days after she vanished, a maintenance worker climbed to the roof and found her — naked, floating face-up, approximately twelve inches from the top of the tank.

21
00:02:13,375 --> 00:02:16,362
The coroner ruled accidental drowning.

22
00:02:16,362 --> 00:02:19,262
No trauma, no foul play.

23
00:02:19,262 --> 00:02:28,675
But Elisa's Tumblr kept posting for weeks after her death — scheduled entries she had written herself, words from a woman who could not have known they would be her last.

24
00:02:28,675 --> 00:02:32,475
The case was officially closed in 2013.

25
00:02:32,475 --> 00:02:34,850
The questions have never been.

